  "worked_solution": "Since $\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}\\cdot Y=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}},$ we have\n\n$$\nY=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}\u00f7\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}\u00f7\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{121y}{2(k+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2(k+2)\\cdot (k+2)}{11y\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot 11y}{2(k+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{2(k+2)}\\cdot (k+2)}{\\cancel{11y}\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot \\cancel{11y}}{\\cancel{2(k+2)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 11 & = \\\\ \\frac{22(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
